# React & Next.js Rules

## Component Patterns

### Functional Components

- ✅ Use functional components with hooks
- ❌ Don't use class-based components

### Hooks

- Follow Rules of Hooks
- Use custom hooks to share logic
- Keep hooks at the top level of components

### Next.js Patterns

- Use Server Components when possible (Next.js 13+)
- Use Client Components only when needed (interactivity, browser APIs)
- Leverage Server Actions for mutations

## State Management

- Use `useState` for local component state
- Use Context for shared state within component tree
- Use external state management (Zustand, Redux) for global state
- Avoid prop drilling - use Context or state management

## Performance

- Use `React.memo` for expensive components
- Use `useMemo` and `useCallback` appropriately
- Implement proper loading and error states
- Use Next.js Image component for optimized images

## DO

- ✅ Use functional components with hooks
- ✅ Use Server Components in Next.js when possible
- ✅ Implement proper loading and error states
- ✅ Use proper key props in lists
- ✅ Memoize expensive computations

## DON'T

- ❌ Use class-based components
- ❌ Mutate state directly
- ❌ Forget to add keys in lists
- ❌ Create components unnecessarily
- ❌ Use Client Components when Server Components will do

## Examples

### Good: Functional Component

```tsx
interface ButtonProps {
  onClick: () => void;
  children: React.ReactNode;
}

export function Button({ onClick, children }: ButtonProps) {
  return (
    <button onClick={onClick}>
      {children}
    </button>
  );
}
```

### Bad: Class Component

```tsx
class Button extends React.Component {
  render() {
    return <button onClick={this.props.onClick}>{this.props.children}</button>;
  }
}
```